;; Brood prelude / runtime contracts — the POLICY HOOK
;; ---------------------------------------------------
;; The sound half of the type system, done Brood-side (the paper's "strong arrow"): a
;; declared signature ENFORCED at every call from outside its module, so a value the static
;; checker trusted can never silently slip through. `(sig …)` in core.blsp is the
;; declaration; whether it is enforced is decided when the name's BINDING comes to exist
;; (ADR-381), by the kernel offering the binding to `%contract-wrap` below — at a `def`, at
;; a `%register-sig` landing on a bound name, in the sweep after a module's bindings arrive
;; from source or from the stdlib image, and over the prelude's own root names at runtime
;; boot. Armed by `BROOD_CONTRACTS=1` (the `nest run`/`nest test` default) or forced per
;; name by `sig!`. The MODULE BOUNDARY (ADR-383) is the kernel's: beside a shim it binds
;; the original under a private alias, and a module's own compiled references to the name
;; read that — a module's calls to its own functions are unchecked; `sig!` has no boundary.
;; See `docs/type-annotations.md`.
;;
;; **This file is the hook and nothing else** (ADR-385). The machinery a shim is MADE of —
;; the spec parsing, the argument/result checks, the templates — lives in `std/contract.blsp`
;; and is loaded by `require-one` at the hook's first ARMED call. Everything in the prelude
;; is localized and frozen into the shared region at every runtime's boot and carried in the
;; prelude image, so 200 lines an unarmed run never reaches were boot cost paid by every
;; process for a mode that is off (KI-182's residual). What stays is what the kernel or the
;; expander needs in every mode: this hook, the exemption, and `sig!`.
;;
;; `%contract-check-op-result` — an ability op's declared return — stays in tools.blsp for a
;; different reason: `defability` names it in the op functions it generates, and the prelude
;; defines abilities of its own, so a qualified reference into a not-yet-loaded module would
;; have to resolve while the prelude is still being built.

;; Two names the MACRO EXPANDER calls at every level of its recursive descent, where a shim
;; costs its frames per level of expansion depth: wrapping `reverse` alone took a cold
;; contracts boot from under 6 MB of stack to over the 12 MB budget (KI-81). Declaration
;; only — the CHECKER still holds callers to `(reverse "ab")`.
(defn- %contract-exempt? (name)
  (or (%eq name 'reverse) (%eq name 'append)))

(defn %contract-wrap (name orig type)
  "The contract policy the kernel consults when the global `name`, declared `type` (an
arrow), comes to hold the closure `orig` (ADR-381): the checking shim to bind instead,
or `orig` itself when nothing is to be enforced — contracts unarmed and the name not
`sig!`-forced, or the name exempt. Called by the kernel, not by programs."
  (if (or (%contract-exempt? name) (not (or (%contracts-armed?) (%contract-forced? name))))
    orig
    (%contract-machinery name orig type)))

;; The `std/contract.blsp` half, reached only when a binding IS to be enforced — once per
;; contracted binding, never on a call path, so the resolution can be as plain as this.
;;
;; **It must not LOAD here.** The module is loaded once at runtime boot when contracts are
;; armed (`Interp::new`), because this hook runs wherever a binding comes to exist — inside
;; a `def`, inside a module's sweep, inside whatever evaluation reached them — and a module
;; load is arbitrary evaluation, hence a collection, at a point those callers are not
;; GC-safe across: it tripped the use-after-GC wire on the tree-walker within a day. The
;; `require-one` below is therefore a no-op in every armed process and a last resort for the
;; one path that arms LATER than boot — a `sig!` in an otherwise unarmed run.
;;
;; `reflect/eval` is a NATIVE under a module prefix, so naming it here loads nothing (a
;; bound name is not a module reference), which a literal `contract/shim` head would not be:
;; with no stdlib image to vouch for the name — the cold prelude build — that head would
;; resolve while the prelude is being built.
(defn- %contract-machinery (name orig type)
  (unless (bound? 'contract/shim) (require-one 'contract))
  ((reflect/eval 'contract/shim) name orig type))

(defmacro sig! (name type)
  "Like `(sig …)` but ENFORCED whatever the mode and at EVERY call — the module's own
calls included, where an armed `(sig …)` guards only the module boundary (ADR-383): each
argument and the result are checked and a mismatch throws a blamed contract error (an
opt-in 'strong arrow'). The checker reads it exactly like `(sig …)`, and like `(sig …)`
it may stand on either side of the definition — the contract is installed when the
binding exists (ADR-381). Supports a `&` rest marker: `(sig! f (int & number ->
string))` checks fixed args then every rest arg, and an `&optional` marker: `(sig! f
(string int &optional string -> string))` checks each optional that was actually supplied
and leaves the callee's own defaults alone. That one shim IS variadic, so `arity-of`
reports `2+` where the function reads `2-3`."
  ;; The declaration first, then the force: `%register-sig` returns the qualified name,
  ;; which is the key the kernel's forced set and its binding both use. Root-scoped
  ;; call, since this expands inside the module being contracted (KI-113).
  `(/%contract-force! (%register-sig '~name '~type)))
